🤖 Agent: Short answer: tea tree oil (Melaleuca alternifolia) has antibacterial, anti-inflammatory, and antifungal properties that can help mild–moderate acne and some other skin issues. It must be diluted and used carefully because neat (undiluted) essential oil commonly irritates or sensitizes skin.

How it helps
- Antibacterial: reduces Cutibacterium acnes (acne bacteria) on the skin.  
- Anti‑inflammatory: calms redness and swelling associated with pimples.  
- Antifungal/antimicrobial: can help fungal conditions (athlete’s foot, mild dandruff) in some cases.

Evidence note
- Clinical trials show topical tea tree oil (around 5%) reduces acne lesions and inflammation, but it works more slowly than benzoyl peroxide and results may be milder.  
- For fungal/dandruff uses there’s some supportive evidence but variable; it’s not a guaranteed cure.

Safe application (step‑by‑step)
1. Patch test first: dilute to a low concentration (see below), apply a small dot to inner forearm, cover, wait 24 hours. If redness/itching/irritation occurs, do NOT use.  
2. Choose a carrier: jojoba oil, grapeseed oil, squalane, or an aloe vera gel base. (Avoid plain coconut oil on acne‑prone skin—it can be comedogenic.)  
3. Target concentration:
   - Sensitive skin or first‑time users: 0.5–1% (very mild).  
   - Typical acne spot treatment: 5% (commonly studied and tolerated).  
   - For stronger antifungal topical use, clinicians sometimes use 5–10%, but start lower and monitor.  
4. Example dilutions by drops (approximate): for a small 20‑drop mixture, 1 drop tea tree + 19 drops carrier ≈ 5%. For 1% use 1 drop tea tree + 99 drops carrier (or use milliliter math below).  
   - Example by volume: 5 mL tea tree + 95 mL carrier = 5% solution.  
5. Apply to clean, dry skin: cleanse first, then apply a small amount of the diluted tea tree to the affected spot(s) with a cotton swab or your finger.  
6. Frequency: once daily at first; if well tolerated, increase to up to twice daily.  
7. Storage: keep in a dark, cool place in a tightly sealed container; essential oils oxidize and can become more irritating with time.

Formulation options
- Spot treat in a gel/aloevera base (5% is common) — pleasant for acne.  
- Add 5% to shampoo for dandruff (many formulations use 2–5%).  
- Use pre‑made, reputable 5% tea tree topical products if you prefer not to mix.

Safety & cautions
- Never apply undiluted (neat) tea tree oil to large skin areas.  
- Avoid eyes, mouth, nostrils, and other mucous membranes.  
- Can cause allergic contact dermatitis in some people—discontinue if rash, intense redness, blistering or burning occurs.  
- Not for ingestion.  
- Pregnancy/breastfeeding: check with your healthcare provider before use.  
- Children: use extreme caution and consult a pediatrician—many guidelines advise avoiding essential oils in very young children.  
- If you have moderate–severe acne, cysts, scarring, or widespread skin disease, see a dermatologist; tea tree oil is not a substitute for prescribed therapies.

Practical tips
- Use tea tree for spot treatment or mild acne; combine with good skincare basics (gentle cleanser, non‑comedogenic moisturizer, sunscreen).  
- Expect slower results than benzoyl peroxide; allow 6–12 weeks to judge effectiveness.  
- If you prefer not to DIY, look for OTC products that specify a 5% tea tree oil formulation and list full ingredients.

If you want, I can:
- Give a simple recipe for a travel‑size 5% tea tree spot serum (exact drops/volumes).  
- Suggest gentle carrier oils or ready‑made product types to look for.  

